# Product identity — what the shipped bundle calls itself.
# Sourced by scripts/build-app.sh and scripts/create-dmg.sh; every value can be
# overridden from the environment, which is how CI injects the release version.
# PRODUCT_NAME reaches the UI as CFBundleName, which is what AppIdentity reads,
# so renaming the app means editing this file and Package.swift and nothing else.
#
# The bundle identifier is permanent in practice: changing it after the first
# release orphans user settings (@AppStorage) and resets the app's Gatekeeper
# reputation. io.github.<user>.<app> is the reverse-DNS form Apple accepts for
# projects without their own domain.

PRODUCT_NAME="${PRODUCT_NAME:-Notchwatch}"
BUNDLE_ID="${BUNDLE_ID:-io.github.sanchpet.notchwatch}"
MIN_MACOS="${MIN_MACOS:-14.0}"
# Shown in the Finder's Get Info panel as NSHumanReadableCopyright. This is the
# one copyright string the product ships, so it must name the same holders and
# years as LICENSE — no more, and no one LICENSE cannot name. LICENSE holds:
#
#   2026 sanchpet                — BSD 3-Clause, the work of this fork
#   2026 the AgentNotch authors  — MIT, the inherited code (upstream ships no
#                                  licence file and names no individual; the
#                                  inherited history runs 2026-01-07..2026-01-19)
#   2025 Kai Azim                — MIT, NotchShape.swift from DynamicNotchKit
#
# Do not put a personal name against the inherited code: none appears anywhere in
# either repository, and inventing one from a signing certificate or a domain is
# how this string went wrong before. AppGram is the upstream GitHub organisation,
# not a copyright holder — do not put it here either. No licence name: LICENSE is
# the authority, and the layout is three licences deep (BSD 3-Clause, inherited
# MIT, MPL-2.0 on CGSSpace.swift).
COPYRIGHT="${COPYRIGHT:-Copyright © 2026 sanchpet. Portions © 2026 the AgentNotch authors, © 2025 Kai Azim. See LICENSE.}"
